  1843	```
  1844	
  1845	- `:ok` is: the name resolves to exactly the pinned address. Several
  1846	  declared principals asserting that one address is `:ok` (R8).
  1847	- `:absent` is: no accepted assertion. Its diagnostics are the fold's for
  1848	  that name, so an assertion by an undeclared principal, or one in an
  1849	  index the reader has not loaded, is told apart from no assertion at all
  1850	  only by what the loaded snapshots hold.
  1851	- `:ambiguous` is: more than one distinct address among accepted
  1852	  assertions, whether or not the pinned one is among them.
  1853	- `:mismatch` is: one address, not the pinned one. A dependency its
  1854	  publisher has since republished is this case.
  1855	- The DHT link source runs the check after the load and before the link
  1856	  (section 4.1). The first entry that is not `:ok` refuses the require with
  1857	  `:yin.link.dht/dependency-binding` carrying that entry. No install child
  1858	  starts.
  1859	- The check is today the **only** guard for a Python import. Discharge
  1860	  (step 5b) compares a pin only through a module obligation, and
  1861	  `declared-obligation` mints one only for a retained free name equal to
  1862	  the required module or qualified by it. A Python import supplies its
  1863	  dependency through a quoted require and module-object operations, not
  1864	  such a retained name, so no module obligation exists and discharge
  1865	  compares nothing. A dependency that reaches the install child some
  1866	  other way (an inherited registry entry, a fresh install) is therefore
  1867	  not checked against its pin after this point.
  1868	- The engine fix is independent of admission and lands with L-d: in the
  1869	  `link-install` `linked` transition, before the installed slice is
  1870	  lifted or published, every pinned require of the manifest is checked
  1871	  against the halted child's linked entry for that name, and an absent or
  1872	  unequal entry refuses `:dependency-pin-mismatch`. Its acceptance
  1873	  bypasses this early guard and covers inherited and freshly installed
  1874	  mismatches on all four VMs.
  1875	
  1876	### 7.5 Admission: the linker's trust question
  1877	
  1878	Status: AD-1 (the seam) and AD-2 (the Python policy) landed in
  1879	`yin.repl.*`. This section moves the responsibility into the linker; the
  1880	AL slices (7.5.14) build it. The binding design is
  1881	`collab/1792262000000-architect-admission-in-linker-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`
  1882	(approved with nits by
  1883	`collab/1792263000000-reviewer-admission-in-linker-r2.findings.md` and
  1884	`collab/1791809519945-reviewer-admission-in-linker-r2-confirm.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`,
  1885	whose nits are folded in below). It supersedes the earlier admission
  1886	design (`collab/1792234000000-architect-admission-stream-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`),
  1887	whose mechanisms it keeps and whose layering it reverses. AD-3 (the REPL
  1888	trust commands) becomes AL-4.
  1889	
  1890	**Pinned by test.** The reason vocabulary of this section, the values of
  1891	`serve-budget` and `pair-capacity` it names, and the retire rule's
  1892	literal `origin + "."` are pinned by `admission-spec-test`
  1893	(`test/yin/repl/admission_doc_test.cljc` today, moved to
  1894	`test/yin/vm/linker/` by AL-1): the test reads this section and fails
  1895	when it and the code diverge. The namespace names
  1896	`yin.vm.linker.interpreter` and `yin.vm.linker.admission` are pinned
  1897	literals of this section too: AL-1 makes the relocated test assert that
  1898	the section names the namespaces whose vars it reads.
  1899	
  1900	Owner directions (2026-10-12). Importing a module over a network is the
  1901	linker's job (`yin.vm.linker`); the REPL gets no machinery for it and
  1902	only configures. A module published by another principal is not refused
  1903	automatically: which principals a holder trusts is a configurable policy,
  1904	and the user of a module may choose to trust its principal. The policy is
  1905	an interpreter of a stream, the shape ShiBi capability tokens already
  1906	have (`dao.shibi.md`). The linker is language-blind (7.5.10).
  1907	
  1908	#### 7.5.1 Layering: admission is the linker's
  1909	
  1910	**The rule.** Linker semantics is what a plain Clojure consumer (section
  1911	10) must have to resolve, ask, bind, deliver, discharge or retire a link.
  1912	The REPL is what disappears with the REPL: cadence, display, retained
  1913	lines, and which principals this session names.
  1914	
  1915	| Lives in | Contents |
  1916	|---|---|
  1917	| `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` (from `yin.repl.link`) | pair minting, `composition`, `authority`, `serve`, `run-descriptor`, `retire`, `formats`, the constants; after AL-2 also `make` and `round`; after AL-3 also `reconfigure` |
  1918	| `yin.vm.linker.admission` (from `yin.repl.admission`) | the protocol, unchanged: `make-state`, `drain`, `ask`, `asked`, `answer-to`, `judge`, `settle`, `forget`, `context`, `trusted-asserter`, `policy`, `interpreter`, `serve`, `reasons` |
  1919	| `yin.vm.engine` / `yin.vm.module` | the `:admission` registry record as landed (7.5.3); the pinned-dependency check (7.4, with L-d) |
  1920	| `yin.repl` | configuration only: `:link-source`, the `:admission` option, `--dht-trust`, `:external`; one `:linker` value after AL-2; one tick hook (7.5.7); the trust commands (AL-4); display text |
  1921	
  1922	Neither linker namespace requires any `yin.repl*` namespace. Follow,
  1923	authority and configuration stay supplied data. AL-1 does the move
  1924	(`git mv` of both namespaces and both admission tests, public API and
  1925	session topology preserved); the old namespaces are deleted with no
  1926	shim. Until AL-1 lands, the code of this section lives at the old names,
  1927	and every `interpreter/` and `admission/` var named below is the
  1928	`yin.repl.link` or `yin.repl.admission` var of the same name.
  1929	
  1930	The request-serving semantics (resolve, ask, bind, deliver, retire)
  1931	exists only in this interpreter, which the VM-level tests
  1932	(`linker_require_test`, `semantic_register/derive_test`) and the Python
  1933	DHT import test already require. `dao.jing.content.driver` is not a
  1934	consumer in that direction: it requires only `dao.jing`,
  1935	`dao.jing.content.step` and `dao.stream`, and only its docstring, which
  1936	names the interpreter as a downstream consumer, changes in AL-1.
  1937	
  1938	#### 7.5.2 The datums
  1939	
  1940	The datum shapes and the reason vocabulary are unchanged by the move.
  1941	
  1942	When the DHT request interpreter resolves a name, it appends an
  1943	admission question to an **admission request medium**. One interpreter
  1944	reads the questions and appends answers to an **admission response
  1945	medium**. The two ring media sit beside the link pair. The admission id
  1946	is `[link-id serial]`. `serial` is a per-composition counter, never
  1947	reused, so a retired question and a later question for the same link id
  1948	never share an id.
  1949	
  1950	```clojure
  1951	;; question, appended to the admission request medium at resolution
  1952	{:yin.admit/id         [[:t0 7] 3]
  1953	 :yin.link/id          [:t0 7]
  1954	 :yin.admit/name       'pym.m
  1955	 :yin.admit/address    :segment/blake3-...   ; the resolved manifest address, retained
  1956	 :yin.admit/asserters  ["ed25519:<hex>" ...] ; every asserter of that entry (R8)
  1957	 :yin.admit/provenance {...}                 ; the entry's :yin.link/provenance verbatim
  1958	 :yin.link/format      f                     ; the request's own words, no subject
  1959	 :yin.link/contract    "b2"}
  1960	
  1961	;; answers, appended to the admission response medium
  1962	{:yin.admit/id [[:t0 7] 3] :yin.admit/decision :allow  :yin.admit/conditions {}}
  1963	{:yin.admit/id [[:t0 7] 3] :yin.admit/decision :refuse
  1964	 :yin.admit/reason :yin.admit/untrusted-principal :yin.admit/asserters [...]}
  1965	```
  1966	
  1967	- The question's key set is closed: exactly the keys above. No session,
  1968	  no subject, no language and no frontend travels in it; the subject is
  1969	  the interpreter's context (7.5.8). No key of the question or of the
  1970	  link request is `:yin.admit/subject`, `:yin.admit/session`, or in the
  1971	  `dao.shibi` namespace. The link request's closed key set
  1972	  (`yin.vm.linker.md` 6.3) is not edited.
  1973	- The allow answer's key set is closed: `:yin.admit/id`,
  1974	  `:yin.admit/decision :allow` and `:yin.admit/conditions`. A refuse
  1975	  answer carries `:yin.admit/id`, `:yin.admit/decision :refuse` and
  1976	  `:yin.admit/reason`, and may carry further keys, which are copied into
  1977	  the link refusal.
  1978	- A direct `:name-env` entry (provenance `:composition`) is not asked.
  1979	  A non-DHT link source has no admission pair.
  1980	- The link response on allow gains `:yin.admit/id`,
  1981	  `:yin.admit/decision :allow`, `:yin.admit/asserters` and
  1982	  `:yin.admit/address`. A refusal copies into the link refusal shape with
  1983	  the answer's reason and its open keys.
  1984	
  1985	**Minting** (AL-2). `(interpreter/make ...)` mints the link pair and,
  1986	only for a `:dht?` source, the admission pair and its answer cursor. A
  1987	local or remote content source has no admission pair. Until AL-2 the
  1988	REPL's `make-session` mints the admission state beside the link pair.
  1989	
  1990	#### 7.5.3 Ask after resolve, before load: the retained entry (F1)
  1991	
  1992	`interpreter/serve` takes `{:pair :source :dht :admission}` and returns
  1993	`{:pair :pending :progress? :dht :admission}`. The admission state is a
  1994	value:
  1995	
  1996	```clojure
  1997	{:pair    {:questions q-medium :answers a-medium :cursor c}  ; c: the composition's answer cursor
  1998	 :asked   {[:t0 7] question}        ; the retained question, at most one per pair in v1
  1999	 :answers {[[:t0 7] 3] answer}      ; drained, keyed by admission id, first answer wins
  2000	 :serial  4}
  2001	```
  2002	
  2003	The question is asked after the name resolves and before any load
  2004	starts. The DHT branch of the link answer takes three cases, in this
  2005	order:
  2006	
  2007	1. **Asked and unanswered.** `:asked` holds an entry for the link id and
  2008	   `:answers` holds nothing under that entry's admission id: the link is
  2009	   pending, and the pending entry carries `:admission <id>` beside
  2010	   `:name` and `:yin.link/id`. The name is not resolved again.
  2011	2. **Asked and answered.** An allow loads the **retained** address, the
  2012	   `:yin.admit/address` of the asked question, never a fresh
  2013	   `resolve-name`. A refuse is the link refusal. When the link response
  2014	   lands, the asked entry and its answer are dropped.
  2015	3. **Not asked.** The name resolves as before. An `:ok` resolution mints
  2016	   the question with the next serial, appends it to the request medium,
  2017	   records it under `:asked`, and reports the link pending with
  2018	   `:admission`. A question append counts as progress. A refused append
  2019	   leaves nothing asked and reports no progress.
  2020	
  2021	**A question-bound link is never refreshed.** Serve stops at the first
  2022	pending request, so at most one question is open per pair, bound to the
  2023	entry it was minted from until an answer lands or the request is retired.
  2024	A head change after the question is asked does not move the address the
  2025	allow loads. If the retained address fails to load after an allow, the
  2026	link is refused as any failed load is, and the asked entry goes with it.
  2027	The user requires again; the new link id gets a new question over the
  2028	head as it then is.
  2029	
  2030	**The registry record.** `engine/link-install` assocs onto the installed
  2031	registry entry, after the module entry is built and before the module is
  2032	received, an `:admission` map `{:yin.admit/id :yin.admit/decision
  2033	:yin.admit/asserters :yin.admit/address}` copied from a response that
  2034	carries `:yin.admit/decision :allow`. Install children inherit entries
  2035	whole and parents receive transferred entries whole, so the record is
  2036	inherited and transferred with them. A direct `:name-env` entry carries
  2037	no `:admission` key.
  2038	
  2039	**Discharge and L-d.** Admission asks after resolve and before load;
  2040	discharge is step 5b after delivery, untouched. An admission refusal is
  2041	a section 9 link refusal, so `try-require` catches it as it catches
  2042	`:absent`. An install child's transitive requirement is asked on its own
  2043	request.
  2044	
  2045	#### 7.5.4 Parking, with no deadline, and retirement
  2046	
  2047	A link whose question is unanswered parks. The wait lives in the link
  2048	pair's own protocol and adds no state: a parked link is a request the
  2049	interpreter's request cursor has not passed, plus a retained question
  2050	under `:asked`. Nothing lands on the link response medium until the
  2051	answer does, so the VM's `:link` wait entry never learns admission
  2052	exists. Nothing times the wait out: there is no clock in admission, and
  2053	liveness belongs to `dao.lease` and to the `:link-policy` (8.3). An
  2054	unanswered question ends only when an answer lands or its request is
  2055	retired by abandonment.
  2056	
  2057	**Abandonment retires the run.** `mint-link-id` emits the current
  2058	counter and then increments it, and child origins are minted the same way
  2059	(origin `:t0.<n>` with `n` the current `:origins`). Both ranges are
  2060	therefore half-open. `(abandon)` computes a run descriptor
  2061	`{:origin :t0 :ids [from until] :children [from until]}` from `base`, the
  2062	parked run's `(:base parked)`, and `parked`, the VM after the abandoned
  2063	installs and links are folded out, with every counter normalized by
  2064	`(or counter 0)`:
  2065	
  2066	- `:ids` is `[(:id-counter base) (:id-counter parked)]`.
  2067	- `:children` is `[(:origins base) (:origins parked)]`.
  2068	
  2069	`(interpreter/retire {:pair :admission} run)` returns
  2070	`{:pair :admission :retired [ids]}`. Walking the link pair's requests with
  2071	`stream/next` from the pair's request cursor, a request with id `[o k]` is
  2072	retired iff:
  2073	
  2074	- `o` is the run's origin and `base <= k < parked-counter`, that is
  2075	  `from <= k < until` of `:ids`; or
  2076	- for some `m` with `from <= m < until` of `:children`, with
  2077	  `child = (str (name origin) "." m)`, `(name o)` equals `child` or starts
  2078	  with `child + "."`, the exact origin or `origin + "."` of that child.
  2079	
  2080	A raw prefix never matches: `"t0.10"` does not start with `"t0.1."`, so
  2081	`:t0.10` is never a descendant of `:t0.1`. Nested install children
  2082	(`:t0.4.0`, `:t0.4.0.2`) match through the `"t0.4."` prefix. Each retired
  2083	request advances the cursor. The first request that is not retired stops
  2084	the walk, keeping serve's stop-at-first-unanswered rule, so a request
  2085	outside the run is never skipped. Retire removes every `:asked` entry
  2086	whose link id was retired and every `:answers` entry under those ids. No
  2087	link response is appended for a retired request: the engine has already
  2088	retired the id, and a response would be `:late`. An answer arriving later
  2089	for a retired id correlates to nothing and the drain drops it.
  2090	
  2091	`:retired` names the run-owned requests the walk still encounters from the
  2092	current cursor, before the first unrelated request. A root request that
  2093	was already answered and spawned an install child is behind the cursor and
  2094	is not in `:retired`; its child's requests are.
  2095	
  2096	`run-descriptor` and `retire` are the interpreter's; `(abandon)` is the
  2097	REPL's command and, after AL-2, makes one `retire` call on the session's
  2098	`:linker` value.
  2099	
  2100	#### 7.5.5 The drain: bounded, consuming, with a persisted cursor
  2101	
  2102	One public `(admission/drain admission budget)` returns the successor
  2103	admission state. It reads the response medium with `stream/next` from
  2104	`:pair :cursor`, at most `budget` elements, and persists the cursor past
  2105	everything it read. Every read consumes; nothing peeks.
  2106	
  2107	- **Admission id comparison is keyed by `[link-id serial]`.** `:asked` is
  2108	  keyed by link id, and an answer by its full admission id. An answer `a`
  2109	  is kept only when its id equals the retained question's id for the
  2110	  answer's link id:
  2111	
  2112	  ```clojure
  2113	  (= (:yin.admit/id a)
  2114	     (:yin.admit/id (get asked (first (:yin.admit/id a)))))
  2115	  ```
  2116	
  2117	  and `:answers` holds nothing under that id yet. An answer with a stale
  2118	  serial for a live link id, a duplicate (the first answer wins), and an
  2119	  answer for an unknown or retired id are consumed and dropped.
  2120	- On `:dao.stream/gap` the drain adopts the recovery cursor the transport
  2121	  returns (the earliest retained position, never an evicted one) and
  2122	  continues within the budget. On `:dao.stream/blocked` or
  2123	  `:dao.stream/end` it stops.
  2124	- Each `round` (7.5.6) drains exactly once, with `serve-budget` (64), at
  2125	  its start. After AL-2 nothing else drains; until then the AD-1 hook
  2126	  `recheck-on-answers` drains with the same function and budget.
  2127	- The media have capacity `pair-capacity` (1024) and evict the oldest
  2128	  element. An answer is lost when the unread backlog on the response
  2129	  medium exceeds that capacity; host composition must bound the
  2130	  accumulated backlog through its production rate and its drain cadence.
  2131	  A lost answer leaves its question pending until retirement. There is no
  2132	  re-answer protocol.
  2133	
  2134	#### 7.5.6 One round
  2135	
  2136	`(interpreter/round linker node budget)` (AL-2) is the linker's unit of
  2137	work over one `:linker` value, in this order:
  2138	
  2139	1. Drain answers: one bounded drain (7.5.5).
  2140	2. Serve link requests, stopping at the first pending one (7.5.3).
  2141	3. Serve the in-process admission interpreter, when the value holds one
  2142	   (7.5.8).
  2143	
  2144	It returns the successor value, the node, the pending entries and a
  2145	progress flag. **Progress** is any of: a link response appended, a
  2146	question appended, an in-process answer appended, or a drained answer
  2147	that matched a retained question. A drained answer that matched nothing
  2148	is consumed and is not progress. The successor is always carried, with
  2149	or without progress: the drain's cursor and the serial move even in a
  2150	round that reports none.
  2151	
  2152	Until AL-2, `drive-links` runs `admission/serve` right after the link
  2153	`serve` and assocs the admission successor before the VM runs; the link
  2154	serve drains at the start of each of its rounds.
  2155	
  2156	#### 7.5.7 The tick-to-VM handoff
  2157	
  2158	A round that delivers a link response advances the request cursor past
  2159	the parked request. If the VM is not then run, the next round finds no
  2160	work and reports no progress, and a driver that runs the VM only on its
  2161	own round's progress strands the delivered response. The handoff closes
  2162	that gap: **the tick retains the round's successor and passes its
  2163	progress into the pending driver, which runs the VM once before
  2164	requiring another round to report progress.**
  2165	
  2166	One host hook, `(recheck-on-tick state events)`, replaces both
  2167	`recheck-on-load-events` and `recheck-on-answers` (AL-2). Per tick:
  2168	
  2169	1. Run one `round` on the session's `:linker` with the session node, and
  2170	   assoc the successor and the node into the state before anything else,
  2171	   so a raising VM run carries them.
  2172	2. If the round reported progress, call the pending driver with that
